{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PvbpbxNGdgAt7QQLi8sbBi","type":"tool_result","content":"# src-tauri/\n\nThe Tauri host. It grows alongside `src/main/` for the whole of Phase 4\nand neither host is a branch (ADR-014); `npm start` stays Electron,\n`npm run start:tauri` is this one.\n\n## The document root is generated, so Cargo depends on npm\n\n`tauri.conf.json` sets `frontendDist` to `../build-tauri`, which\n`bin/build-tauri.js` assembles by copying `static/` and `build-vite/`\nside by side. That layout is not cosmetic: `static/main.html` is shared\nwith Electron and reaches the bundle through a relative\n`<script src=\"../build-vite/renderer/main.js\">`, so the two directories\nhave to be siblings inside the root or that `..` escapes it. Tauri\nwalks the root at compile time to embed it and does not follow\nsymlinks, so it has to be a copy.\n\nThe consequence for every Cargo command: **`cargo build`, `cargo test`\nand `cargo clippy` all panic if `build-tauri/` does not exist**\n(`tauri::generate_context!` expands the directory at compile time).\nRun `npm run build:tauri` first in a fresh clone. For the same reason a\nrenderer change needs `npm run build:tauri` before the Rust binary\nserves it -- `cargo run` alone reuses the embedded copy.\n\n## The renderer in that root is built for *this* host\n\nSince US-052 `npm run build:tauri` runs\n`npm run build:renderer:tauri`, not `npm run build:renderer`: the\nformer sets `WT_HOST=tauri`, which swaps\n`src/renderer/lib/platform/*.js` for `src/renderer/lib/platform/tauri/*.js`\nin the bundle (ADR-014). Assembling the document root from a plain\n`npm run build:renderer` gives a window full of Electron shim code\nthat cannot load, so use the npm script rather than running\n`bin/build-tauri.js` by hand after some other build.\n\nMost of that Tauri tree throws `NotImplementedError` today, on purpose\n-- each Phase 4 story implements one module. A window that comes up and\nreports not-implemented is the expected state, not a regression.\n\n## Path resolution is a pure function of an `Env`\n\n`src/paths.rs` mirrors the path block of `src/config.js`. Keep the two\nin sync: `AppPaths::resolve` reads only the [`Env`] struct it is\nhanded, never process state, so `Env::current` is the one place that\ntouches env vars, `current_exe` and the disk. That is what lets a test\nassert the Windows portable layout from macOS -- follow the pattern\nwhen adding a host concern that varies by platform rather than\nreaching for `cfg!(target_os)` in the logic.\n\n`matches_src_config_js_on_this_platform` shells out to `node\n--input-type=module -e \"import('./src/config.js')...\"` from the repo\nroot and compares the answers, so a layout change made on the JS side\nalone fails `cargo test`. Node loads `src/config.js` as real ESM there\n(the same path the `bin/` scripts take), and `IS_PRODUCTION` is false\nunder plain Node, so it takes the non-portable branch a debug `cargo\ntest` also takes. Do not add a comparison of\n`DEFAULT_DOWNLOAD_PATH`: `getPath()` in `config.js` returns `''`\noutside an Electron process.\n\n`is_production` here is `!cfg!(debug_assertions)`, the closest analogue\nof Electron's \"am I a packaged binary\" test. A debug build therefore\nnever looks portable, which also means the portable branch cannot be\nexercised by running the dev binary -- assert it through `Env` instead.\n\n## The window is opened by the host, not by the config\n\n`tauri.conf.json` declares the window with `\"visible\": false`, the way\n`src/main/windows/main.js` passes `show: false`. Everything about where\nit opens is decided at runtime in `src/window.rs` and applied by\n`restore()` in `main.rs`: the saved rectangle out of the renderer's\n`config.json`, the minimum size, the maximized flag and whether a\n`--hidden` launch shows it at all. Set the declared window visible\nagain and all four are lost -- it comes up at the default size before\nany of them is applied.\n\n`src/window.rs` follows `paths.rs`: the decisions are pure functions of\nplain values (`WindowLayout::resolve`, `logical_bounds`, `is_hidden`),\nand `main.rs` is the only part that touches a window. One conversion is\nnot optional -- Tauri reports **physical** pixels and Electron's\n`getBounds()` is **logical**, so a rectangle read off the window goes\nthrough `logical_bounds` before the renderer sees it, or the saved size\ndoubles on every restart on a retina display.\n\n## Anything the renderer calls needs a capability\n\n`capabilities/default.json` grants `core:default` to the `main` window.\nApp-defined `#[tauri::command]`s need no entry, but **core APIs the\nrenderer reaches through `@tauri-apps/api` do** -- event subscription\nincluded. Without that file the shim's listener exports fail at\nruntime with a permission error and nothing else looks wrong.\n\nThe same is true of a **plugin**, and it cuts the other way too: a\nplugin's JS API (`@tauri-apps/plugin-dialog` and friends) needs its\npermissions granted to the whole webview, while its *Rust* API does\nnot. So `register the plugin, wrap it in a command` is the cheaper\nshape -- US-058's dialogs are `tauri_plugin_dialog::init()` plus\n`show_open_dialog` / `show_save_dialog` in `main.rs`, and the renderer\nstill carries nothing but `core:default`. Two things to know when\nfollowing it: the plugin's own `blocking_*` helpers deadlock the event\nloop on the main thread, so the command that calls them must be\n`async` (which is what puts it on the async runtime), and a plugin adds\nits ACL manifest to the regenerated `gen/schemas/`, which is\ngitignored -- nothing to commit.\n\n`async` is the safer default for any plugin command generally, not just\nfor the `blocking_*` pickers: the clipboard plugin's `read_text` is\n`arboard`'s, which deadlocks on Linux if it runs on the main thread\nwhile reading something the webview itself copied.\n\n## File access is a host allowlist, not a capability scope\n\n`src/fs.rs` holds the directories the renderer may reach, and every\n`fs_*` command in `main.rs` starts by resolving its path through it.\nThree things to keep true when touching it:\n\n- **A command operates on what `Allowlist::resolve` returns**, never on\n  the string it was given. The returned path has had `..` taken out and\n  its existing ancestors canonicalized; using the original would leave\n  the check and the operation looking at different files.\n- **A new root needs its reason written down** in the table at the top\n  of `src/fs.rs` and in `docs/platform-shim.md`. \"Exactly the\n  directories the shim needs\" is a claim the two documents make on the\n  code's behalf.\n- **A host concern that learns a path the user chose should grant it.**\n  The dialog commands do (a folder for writing, a file for reading);\n  the drag-and-drop delivery in US-065 will have to.\n\n`capabilities/default.json` stays at `core:default` through all of\nthis, which is the point: the webview has no file access of its own to\nmisuse.\n\n## An ordinary crate can outrun the declared MSRV\n\n`rust-version` is 1.77.2 (ADR-014), and Cargo's resolver does not\nhonour it -- `cargo add` warns about a too-new version and then the\nlockfile picks it anyway. So a non-Tauri dependency needs its\n`rust-version` checked, and pinning exactly (`trash = \"=5.2.3\"`, US-059)\nis how the declaration stays true. Note *why* in `Cargo.toml`, because\nthe next person's instinct is to unpin it.\n\n## The tray decides whether closing the window ends the app\n\n`src/tray.rs` is `src/main/tray.js`, and like the menus it is data\nfirst: `is_supported`, `template`, `action` and `on_close` are pure and\nasserted from any platform. Three things follow from it that are not\nlocal to the file:\n\n- **The tray is what makes a close a hide.** `on_close` is\n  `win.on('close')` in `src/main/windows/main.js`, and it needs the\n  `QUITTING` flag in `main.rs` -- Electron's `app.isQuitting` -- or the\n  quit the tray asks for is swallowed by the close handler that hides.\n  Anything that ends the app must go through `quit()`, and\n  `RunEvent::ExitRequested` sets the same flag for the exits that do\n  not.\n- **The tray icon is embedded, not read.** The document root is\n  compiled into the binary, so there is no `static/WebTorrent.png` on\n  disk in a packaged build; `icon_bytes` is `include_bytes!` and the\n  `image-png` / `image-ico` features on the `tauri` dependency are\n  what decode it. `tray-icon` is the feature for the tray itself.\n- **The menu is replaced, never edited.** The one item's label follows\n  the window, exactly as `updateTrayMenu` rebuilds the template, so\n  `refresh` builds a new menu and calls `set_menu`. Every path that\n  shows or hides the window has to call it.\n\n## Driving a real tray without a human (macOS)\n\nmacOS gets no tray at all, which leaves the whole module unobservable\non the development machine. A throwaway `std::env::var(\"WT_FORCE_TRAY\")`\nescape hatch in `tray::init` plus `osascript` covers it, and unlike the\napplication menu the reads here were **not** stale -- the item names\ncame back changed immediately after the window was hidden:\n\n```\nosascript -e 'tell application \"System Events\" to tell process \\\n  \"webtorrent-desktop\"\nclick menu bar item 1 of menu bar 2\ndelay 1\nname of every menu item of menu 1 of menu bar item 1 of menu bar 2\nend tell'\n```\n\nA status item lives in `menu bar 2` (`menu bar 1` is the app's own),\n`click menu item \"Hide to tray\" of menu 1 of ...` fires it, `count of\nwindows` is whether the window is showing, and `click button 1 of\nwindow 1` is the close button. Revert the escape hatch before\ncommitting.\n\n## The sidecar is a second process, and the pipe is what stops an orphan\n\n`src/engine.rs` is `src/main/engine-process.js`: it spawns\n`build/engine/index.js` under `node`, reads the port off the one\n`WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT=` stdout line, restarts on a crash with the\nsame 500ms-doubling-to-8s policy, and answers `get_engine_endpoint`\nwith `{ url, token }`. Three things about it are not local to the file:\n\n- **`npm run build:tauri` runs `build:main` for this reason.** The\n  sidecar is the Babel output of `src/engine/`, and nothing else in the\n  Tauri build ever needed `build/`. Leave it out and the window comes\n  up with no torrenting and no build error.\n- **The child is stopped by closing its stdin, never by a signal.** It\n  is spawned with `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PARENT_PIPE=1`, which is what\n  makes the pipe closing a shutdown request -- so it releases the\n  download directory on a clean quit, *and* dies on its own when the\n  host is force quit and never reaches `RunEvent::Exit`. That is why\n  the module is `std`-only and identical on Windows;\n  `Child::kill` is only the backstop after four seconds.\n- **`CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` is what finds the repo in a dev build.** It is\n  baked in at compile time, which is exactly why the packaged branch of\n  `Runtime::resolve` uses the resource directory instead -- filling\n  that directory is US-047.\n\nChecking it needs no UI: launch the debug binary (with a throwaway\n`HOME`, below) and the child's own output arrives on the host's stdout\nas `Engine: ...` lines, port included. `lsof -nP -iTCP:<port>` says\nwhether anything connected, and `pgrep -f build/engine/index.js` after\nthe host is gone is the orphan check -- run it for both quits, a\n`pkill` of the host (the force-quit path, where only the pipe can\nwork) and a real Cmd-Q through `osascript`.\n\n## The external player is a child nobody reaps\n\n`src/external_player.rs` is `src/main/external-player.js` plus the\n`vlc-command` package, which is not a dependency any more. Three things\nabout it are not local to the file:\n\n- **A spawned player outlives the host, on purpose.** Nothing kills it\n  on `RunEvent::Exit` -- Electron leaves it running too, and a check\n  confirmed VLC still playing after the host was killed. The only kill\n  is `quit_external_player`, and it is silent: the renderer asked, so\n  the renderer is not told.\n- **`check_for_external_player` and `open_external_player` are\n  `async`.** The lookup runs a `--version` probe per candidate, each\n  one a process, and `open` also hands the media keys back, which\n  deadlocks from a synchronous command (see the global-shortcut entry\n  below). Both do their blocking on `spawn_blocking`.\n- **`vlc-command`'s Linux list contains a command line, not a path.**\n  `flatpak run org.videolan.VLC` is spawned here as a program plus two\n  arguments; spawning the whole string, as the JS does, is an `ENOENT`\n  upstream that only the shell-backed `--version` probe hid. Only that\n  one entry goes through `Launch::parse`: splitting the rest on\n  whitespace would turn a home directory with a space in it into a\n  program and an argument.\n\n## Driving a real player, or anything that needs a stream\n\nA host feature that hands a URL to another application can be checked\nend to end without a torrent: `say -o probe.aiff \"$(python3 -c \"print('a\nsentence. '*40)\")\"` makes a media file a couple of minutes long,\n`python3 -m http.server 8765 --bind 127.0.0.1` serves it out of that\ndirectory with an access log, and the probe passes that URL instead of\n    })\n    .await\n    .map_err(|err| err.to_string())\n}\n\n/// `ipcOs.quitExternalPlayer`: the renderer is leaving the player.\n///\n/// Killing is silent -- the process going away this way dispatches\n/// nothing, because the renderer is the one that asked. Synchronous\n/// because a `SIGKILL` and its reaping are immediate; there is nothing\n/// here to wait for.\n#[tauri::command]\nfn quit_external_player(external_player: tauri::State<'_, ExternalPlayer>) {\n    external_player.kill();\n}\n\n// -- the dock and the taskbar (US-063) -------------------------------\n//\n// `src/main/dock.js`, minus the progress bar, which is the window's\n// own and sits with the other window commands above. Which of these\n// does anything is a per-platform decision, made in [`crate::dock`]\n// and asserted for all three platforms there; what is left here is the\n// call each decision leads to.\n\n/// `ipcOs.setBadge`: the download counter on the dock icon.\n#[tauri::command]\nfn set_badge(main: WebviewWindow, count: f64) -> Result<(), String> {\n    match dock::badge(Platform::current(), count) {\n        Badge::Show(count) => main.set_badge_count(Some(count)).map_err(failed),\n        Badge::Clear => main.set_badge_count(None).map_err(failed),\n        Badge::Unsupported => Ok(()),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `ipcOs.notifyDownloadFinished`: bounce the Downloads stack if the\n/// file landed in it.\n///\n/// The Dock decides whether the path is somewhere it draws a stack\n/// for, exactly as it does under Electron -- all that is sent is the\n/// path. Posting the notification is AppKit, so it goes to the main\n/// thread rather than running on the command's worker thread.\n#[tauri::command]\nfn download_finished(app: AppHandle, path: String) -> Result<(), String> {\n    if dock::download_notice(Platform::current()) == DownloadNotice::Unsupported {\n        return Ok(());\n    }\n\n    app.run_on_main_thread(move || dock::bounce_downloads_stack(&path))\n        .map_err(failed)\n}\n\n/// `ipcMain.on('startFolderWatcher')`.\n///\n/// Takes nothing, exactly as the Electron handler does: which folder\n/// to watch is the user's `torrentsFolderPath` preference, and the\n/// host reads the saved state for itself rather than being told\n/// (`src/main/saved-state.js`).\n#[tauri::command]\nfn start_folder_watcher(app: AppHandle) {\n    watch_folder(&app);\n}\n\n/// `ipcMain.on('stopFolderWatcher')`.\n#[tauri::command]\nfn stop_folder_watcher(folder_watcher: tauri::State<'_, FolderWatcher>) {\n    folder_watcher.stop();\n}\n\n/// `ipcMain.on('getEngineEndpoint')`: where the engine sidecar is\n/// listening, and the credential it expects (US-070).\n///\n/// The renderer asks on every connect, because the port changes across\n/// a restart. `None` is the same answer `src/main/ipc.js` sends when\n/// the supervisor has nothing to report: not an error, and something\n/// `ipc-engine.js` retries.\n///\n/// `async` because it waits: the UI may ask before the first spawn has\n/// finished, or during the gap while a restart is in flight, and a\n/// synchronous command would do that waiting on the main thread. The\n/// wait itself is a blocking one on a condition variable, so it goes\n/// to `spawn_blocking` rather than tying up an async worker.\n#[tauri::command]\nasync fn get_engine_endpoint(engine: tauri::State<'_, Engine>) -> Result<Option<Endpoint>, String> {\n    let engine = engine.inner().clone();\n    tauri::async_runtime::spawn_blocking(move || engine.wait_for_endpoint(engine::ENDPOINT_TIMEOUT))\n        .await\n        .map_err(|err| err.to_string())\n}\n\n/// `ipcMain.on('setStartup')`: turn launching at login on or off,\n/// which is `install()` or `uninstall()` in `src/main/startup.js`.\n///\n/// `async` because the work is a file write on macOS and Linux and a\n/// `reg.exe` child on Windows; a synchronous command would do either\n/// on the main thread with the UI waiting on it.\n#[tauri::command]\nasync fn set_startup(startup: bool) -> Result<(), String> {\n    let Some(entry) = startup::Entry::current() else {\n        return Err(\"cannot tell which executable to register\".to_string());\n    };\n\n1659:            app.manage(paths);\n1660:            app.manage(watch);\n1661-            // Nothing is playing yet, so it starts holding nothing\n1662-            // (US-066).\n1663:            app.manage(PowerSaveBlocker::platform());\n1664-            // Nothing is playing yet, so the media keys stay with\n1665-            // whatever else on the machine wants them (US-067).\n1666:            app.manage(MediaShortcuts::global(app.handle().clone()));\n1667:            app.manage(allowlist);\n1668:            app.manage(app_menu);\n1669:            app.manage(ContextMenus::<Wry>::default());\n1670:            app.manage(FolderWatcher::default());\n1671-            // Nothing is looked for and nothing is spawned until the\n1672-            // renderer asks (US-080).\n1673:            app.manage(ExternalPlayer::system());\n1674-            // Nothing is discovered and nothing is connected until the\n1675-            // renderer asks (US-075).\n1676:            app.manage(cast::Session::default());\n1677-\n1678-            // The engine sidecar (US-070), which is\n1679-            // `engineProcess.init()` in `src/main/index.js`: it comes\n--\n1700:            app.manage(engine);\n1701-\n1702-            // `delayedInit` in `src/main/index.js`: the watcher comes\n1703-            // up with the app when the preference is already on, so\n--\n1722:        .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![\n1723-            app_paths,\n1724-            window_is_visible,\n1725-            window_is_maximized,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67494884-68da-4440-a200-b8d24d5f1da3","uuid":"51f808d6-5855-4f43-a636-d2d6e4cec9f6","timestamp":"2026-10-13T05:21:46.368Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# src-tauri/\n\nThe Tauri host. It grows alongside `src/main/` for the whole of Phase 4\nand neither host is a branch (ADR-014); `npm start` stays Electron,\n`npm run start:tauri` is this one.\n\n## The document root is generated, so Cargo depends on npm\n\n`tauri.conf.json` sets `frontendDist` to `../build-tauri`, which\n`bin/build-tauri.js` assembles by copying `static/` and `build-vite/`\nside by side. That layout is not cosmetic: `static/main.html` is shared\nwith Electron and reaches the bundle through a relative\n`<script src=\"../build-vite/renderer/main.js\">`, so the two directories\nhave to be siblings inside the root or that `..` escapes it. Tauri\nwalks the root at compile time to embed it and does not follow\nsymlinks, so it has to be a copy.\n\nThe consequence for every Cargo command: **`cargo build`, `cargo test`\nand `cargo clippy` all panic if `build-tauri/` does not exist**\n(`tauri::generate_context!` expands the directory at compile time).\nRun `npm run build:tauri` first in a fresh clone. For the same reason a\nrenderer change needs `npm run build:tauri` before the Rust binary\nserves it -- `cargo run` alone reuses the embedded copy.\n\n## The renderer in that root is built for *this* host\n\nSince US-052 `npm run build:tauri` runs\n`npm run build:renderer:tauri`, not `npm run build:renderer`: the\nformer sets `WT_HOST=tauri`, which swaps\n`src/renderer/lib/platform/*.js` for `src/renderer/lib/platform/tauri/*.js`\nin the bundle (ADR-014). Assembling the document root from a plain\n`npm run build:renderer` gives a window full of Electron shim code\nthat cannot load, so use the npm script rather than running\n`bin/build-tauri.js` by hand after some other build.\n\nMost of that Tauri tree throws `NotImplementedError` today, on purpose\n-- each Phase 4 story implements one module. A window that comes up and\nreports not-implemented is the expected state, not a regression.\n\n## Path resolution is a pure function of an `Env`\n\n`src/paths.rs` mirrors the path block of `src/config.js`. Keep the two\nin sync: `AppPaths::resolve` reads only the [`Env`] struct it is\nhanded, never process state, so `Env::current` is the one place that\ntouches env vars, `current_exe` and the disk. That is what lets a test\nassert the Windows portable layout from macOS -- follow the pattern\nwhen adding a host concern that varies by platform rather than\nreaching for `cfg!(target_os)` in the logic.\n\n`matches_src_config_js_on_this_platform` shells out to `node\n--input-type=module -e \"import('./src/config.js')...\"` from the repo\nroot and compares the answers, so a layout change made on the JS side\nalone fails `cargo test`. Node loads `src/config.js` as real ESM there\n(the same path the `bin/` scripts take), and `IS_PRODUCTION` is false\nunder plain Node, so it takes the non-portable branch a debug `cargo\ntest` also takes. Do not add a comparison of\n`DEFAULT_DOWNLOAD_PATH`: `getPath()` in `config.js` returns `''`\noutside an Electron process.\n\n`is_production` here is `!cfg!(debug_assertions)`, the closest analogue\nof Electron's \"am I a packaged binary\" test. A debug build therefore\nnever looks portable, which also means the portable branch cannot be\nexercised by running the dev binary -- assert it through `Env` instead.\n\n## The window is opened by the host, not by the config\n\n`tauri.conf.json` declares the window with `\"visible\": false`, the way\n`src/main/windows/main.js` passes `show: false`. Everything about where\nit opens is decided at runtime in `src/window.rs` and applied by\n`restore()` in `main.rs`: the saved rectangle out of the renderer's\n`config.json`, the minimum size, the maximized flag and whether a\n`--hidden` launch shows it at all. Set the declared window visible\nagain and all four are lost -- it comes up at the default size before\nany of them is applied.\n\n`src/window.rs` follows `paths.rs`: the decisions are pure functions of\nplain values (`WindowLayout::resolve`, `logical_bounds`, `is_hidden`),\nand `main.rs` is the only part that touches a window. One conversion is\nnot optional -- Tauri reports **physical** pixels and Electron's\n`getBounds()` is **logical**, so a rectangle read off the window goes\nthrough `logical_bounds` before the renderer sees it, or the saved size\ndoubles on every restart on a retina display.\n\n## Anything the renderer calls needs a capability\n\n`capabilities/default.json` grants `core:default` to the `main` window.\nApp-defined `#[tauri::command]`s need no entry, but **core APIs the\nrenderer reaches through `@tauri-apps/api` do** -- event subscription\nincluded. Without that file the shim's listener exports fail at\nruntime with a permission error and nothing else looks wrong.\n\nThe same is true of a **plugin**, and it cuts the other way too: a\nplugin's JS API (`@tauri-apps/plugin-dialog` and friends) needs its\npermissions granted to the whole webview, while its *Rust* API does\nnot. So `register the plugin, wrap it in a command` is the cheaper\nshape -- US-058's dialogs are `tauri_plugin_dialog::init()` plus\n`show_open_dialog` / `show_save_dialog` in `main.rs`, and the renderer\nstill carries nothing but `core:default`. Two things to know when\nfollowing it: the plugin's own `blocking_*` helpers deadlock the event\nloop on the main thread, so the command that calls them must be\n`async` (which is what puts it on the async runtime), and a plugin adds\nits ACL manifest to the regenerated `gen/schemas/`, which is\ngitignored -- nothing to commit.\n\n`async` is the safer default for any plugin command generally, not just\nfor the `blocking_*` pickers: the clipboard plugin's `read_text` is\n`arboard`'s, which deadlocks on Linux if it runs on the main thread\nwhile reading something the webview itself copied.\n\n## File access is a host allowlist, not a capability scope\n\n`src/fs.rs` holds the directories the renderer may reach, and every\n`fs_*` command in `main.rs` starts by resolving its path through it.\nThree things to keep true when touching it:\n\n- **A command operates on what `Allowlist::resolve` returns**, never on\n  the string it was given. The returned path has had `..` taken out and\n  its existing ancestors canonicalized; using the original would leave\n  the check and the operation looking at different files.\n- **A new root needs its reason written down** in the table at the top\n  of `src/fs.rs` and in `docs/platform-shim.md`. \"Exactly the\n  directories the shim needs\" is a claim the two documents make on the\n  code's behalf.\n- **A host concern that learns a path the user chose should grant it.**\n  The dialog commands do (a folder for writing, a file for reading);\n  the drag-and-drop delivery in US-065 will have to.\n\n`capabilities/default.json` stays at `core:default` through all of\nthis, which is the point: the webview has no file access of its own to\nmisuse.\n\n## An ordinary crate can outrun the declared MSRV\n\n`rust-version` is 1.77.2 (ADR-014), and Cargo's resolver does not\nhonour it -- `cargo add` warns about a too-new version and then the\nlockfile picks it anyway. So a non-Tauri dependency needs its\n`rust-version` checked, and pinning exactly (`trash = \"=5.2.3\"`, US-059)\nis how the declaration stays true. Note *why* in `Cargo.toml`, because\nthe next person's instinct is to unpin it.\n\n## The tray decides whether closing the window ends the app\n\n`src/tray.rs` is `src/main/tray.js`, and like the menus it is data\nfirst: `is_supported`, `template`, `action` and `on_close` are pure and\nasserted from any platform. Three things follow from it that are not\nlocal to the file:\n\n- **The tray is what makes a close a hide.** `on_close` is\n  `win.on('close')` in `src/main/windows/main.js`, and it needs the\n  `QUITTING` flag in `main.rs` -- Electron's `app.isQuitting` -- or the\n  quit the tray asks for is swallowed by the close handler that hides.\n  Anything that ends the app must go through `quit()`, and\n  `RunEvent::ExitRequested` sets the same flag for the exits that do\n  not.\n- **The tray icon is embedded, not read.** The document root is\n  compiled into the binary, so there is no `static/WebTorrent.png` on\n  disk in a packaged build; `icon_bytes` is `include_bytes!` and the\n  `image-png` / `image-ico` features on the `tauri` dependency are\n  what decode it. `tray-icon` is the feature for the tray itself.\n- **The menu is replaced, never edited.** The one item's label follows\n  the window, exactly as `updateTrayMenu` rebuilds the template, so\n  `refresh` builds a new menu and calls `set_menu`. Every path that\n  shows or hides the window has to call it.\n\n## Driving a real tray without a human (macOS)\n\nmacOS gets no tray at all, which leaves the whole module unobservable\non the development machine. A throwaway `std::env::var(\"WT_FORCE_TRAY\")`\nescape hatch in `tray::init` plus `osascript` covers it, and unlike the\napplication menu the reads here were **not** stale -- the item names\ncame back changed immediately after the window was hidden:\n\n```\nosascript -e 'tell application \"System Events\" to tell process \\\n  \"webtorrent-desktop\"\nclick menu bar item 1 of menu bar 2\ndelay 1\nname of every menu item of menu 1 of menu bar item 1 of menu bar 2\nend tell'\n```\n\nA status item lives in `menu bar 2` (`menu bar 1` is the app's own),\n`click menu item \"Hide to tray\" of menu 1 of ...` fires it, `count of\nwindows` is whether the window is showing, and `click button 1 of\nwindow 1` is the close button. Revert the escape hatch before\ncommitting.\n\n## The sidecar is a second process, and the pipe is what stops an orphan\n\n`src/engine.rs` is `src/main/engine-process.js`: it spawns\n`build/engine/index.js` under `node`, reads the port off the one\n`WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT=` stdout line, restarts on a crash with the\nsame 500ms-doubling-to-8s policy, and answers `get_engine_endpoint`\nwith `{ url, token }`. Three things about it are not local to the file:\n\n- **`npm run build:tauri` runs `build:main` for this reason.** The\n  sidecar is the Babel output of `src/engine/`, and nothing else in the\n  Tauri build ever needed `build/`. Leave it out and the window comes\n  up with no torrenting and no build error.\n- **The child is stopped by closing its stdin, never by a signal.** It\n  is spawned with `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PARENT_PIPE=1`, which is what\n  makes the pipe closing a shutdown request -- so it releases the\n  download directory on a clean quit, *and* dies on its own when the\n  host is force quit and never reaches `RunEvent::Exit`. That is why\n  the module is `std`-only and identical on Windows;\n  `Child::kill` is only the backstop after four seconds.\n- **`CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` is what finds the repo in a dev build.** It is\n  baked in at compile time, which is exactly why the packaged branch of\n  `Runtime::resolve` uses the resource directory instead -- filling\n  that directory is US-047.\n\nChecking it needs no UI: launch the debug binary (with a throwaway\n`HOME`, below) and the child's own output arrives on the host's stdout\nas `Engine: ...` lines, port included. `lsof -nP -iTCP:<port>` says\nwhether anything connected, and `pgrep -f build/engine/index.js` after\nthe host is gone is the orphan check -- run it for both quits, a\n`pkill` of the host (the force-quit path, where only the pipe can\nwork) and a real Cmd-Q through `osascript`.\n\n## The external player is a child nobody reaps\n\n`src/external_player.rs` is `src/main/external-player.js` plus the\n`vlc-command` package, which is not a dependency any more. Three things\nabout it are not local to the file:\n\n- **A spawned player outlives the host, on purpose.** Nothing kills it\n  on `RunEvent::Exit` -- Electron leaves it running too, and a check\n  confirmed VLC still playing after the host was killed. The only kill\n  is `quit_external_player`, and it is silent: the renderer asked, so\n  the renderer is not told.\n- **`check_for_external_player` and `open_external_player` are\n  `async`.** The lookup runs a `--version` probe per candidate, each\n  one a process, and `open` also hands the media keys back, which\n  deadlocks from a synchronous command (see the global-shortcut entry\n  below). Both do their blocking on `spawn_blocking`.\n- **`vlc-command`'s Linux list contains a command line, not a path.**\n  `flatpak run org.videolan.VLC` is spawned here as a program plus two\n  arguments; spawning the whole string, as the JS does, is an `ENOENT`\n  upstream that only the shell-backed `--version` probe hid. Only that\n  one entry goes through `Launch::parse`: splitting the rest on\n  whitespace would turn a home directory with a space in it into a\n  program and an argument.\n\n## Driving a real player, or anything that needs a stream\n\nA host feature that hands a URL to another application can be checked\nend to end without a torrent: `say -o probe.aiff \"$(python3 -c \"print('a\nsentence. '*40)\")\"` makes a media file a couple of minutes long,\n`python3 -m http.server 8765 --bind 127.0.0.1` serves it out of that\ndirectory with an access log, and the probe passes that URL instead of\n    })\n    .await\n    .map_err(|err| err.to_string())\n}\n\n/// `ipcOs.quitExternalPlayer`: the renderer is leaving the player.\n///\n/// Killing is silent -- the process going away this way dispatches\n/// nothing, because the renderer is the one that asked. Synchronous\n/// because a `SIGKILL` and its reaping are immediate; there is nothing\n/// here to wait for.\n#[tauri::command]\nfn quit_external_player(external_player: tauri::State<'_, ExternalPlayer>) {\n    external_player.kill();\n}\n\n// -- the dock and the taskbar (US-063) -------------------------------\n//\n// `src/main/dock.js`, minus the progress bar, which is the window's\n// own and sits with the other window commands above. Which of these\n// does anything is a per-platform decision, made in [`crate::dock`]\n// and asserted for all three platforms there; what is left here is the\n// call each decision leads to.\n\n/// `ipcOs.setBadge`: the download counter on the dock icon.\n#[tauri::command]\nfn set_badge(main: WebviewWindow, count: f64) -> Result<(), String> {\n    match dock::badge(Platform::current(), count) {\n        Badge::Show(count) => main.set_badge_count(Some(count)).map_err(failed),\n        Badge::Clear => main.set_badge_count(None).map_err(failed),\n        Badge::Unsupported => Ok(()),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `ipcOs.notifyDownloadFinished`: bounce the Downloads stack if the\n/// file landed in it.\n///\n/// The Dock decides whether the path is somewhere it draws a stack\n/// for, exactly as it does under Electron -- all that is sent is the\n/// path. Posting the notification is AppKit, so it goes to the main\n/// thread rather than running on the command's worker thread.\n#[tauri::command]\nfn download_finished(app: AppHandle, path: String) -> Result<(), String> {\n    if dock::download_notice(Platform::current()) == DownloadNotice::Unsupported {\n        return Ok(());\n    }\n\n    app.run_on_main_thread(move || dock::bounce_downloads_stack(&path))\n        .map_err(failed)\n}\n\n/// `ipcMain.on('startFolderWatcher')`.\n///\n/// Takes nothing, exactly as the Electron handler does: which folder\n/// to watch is the user's `torrentsFolderPath` preference, and the\n/// host reads the saved state for itself rather than being told\n/// (`src/main/saved-state.js`).\n#[tauri::command]\nfn start_folder_watcher(app: AppHandle) {\n    watch_folder(&app);\n}\n\n/// `ipcMain.on('stopFolderWatcher')`.\n#[tauri::command]\nfn stop_folder_watcher(folder_watcher: tauri::State<'_, FolderWatcher>) {\n    folder_watcher.stop();\n}\n\n/// `ipcMain.on('getEngineEndpoint')`: where the engine sidecar is\n/// listening, and the credential it expects (US-070).\n///\n/// The renderer asks on every connect, because the port changes across\n/// a restart. `None` is the same answer `src/main/ipc.js` sends when\n/// the supervisor has nothing to report: not an error, and something\n/// `ipc-engine.js` retries.\n///\n/// `async` because it waits: the UI may ask before the first spawn has\n/// finished, or during the gap while a restart is in flight, and a\n/// synchronous command would do that waiting on the main thread. The\n/// wait itself is a blocking one on a condition variable, so it goes\n/// to `spawn_blocking` rather than tying up an async worker.\n#[tauri::command]\nasync fn get_engine_endpoint(engine: tauri::State<'_, Engine>) -> Result<Option<Endpoint>, String> {\n    let engine = engine.inner().clone();\n    tauri::async_runtime::spawn_blocking(move || engine.wait_for_endpoint(engine::ENDPOINT_TIMEOUT))\n        .await\n        .map_err(|err| err.to_string())\n}\n\n/// `ipcMain.on('setStartup')`: turn launching at login on or off,\n/// which is `install()` or `uninstall()` in `src/main/startup.js`.\n///\n/// `async` because the work is a file write on macOS and Linux and a\n/// `reg.exe` child on Windows; a synchronous command would do either\n/// on the main thread with the UI waiting on it.\n#[tauri::command]\nasync fn set_startup(startup: bool) -> Result<(), String> {\n    let Some(entry) = startup::Entry::current() else {\n        return Err(\"cannot tell which executable to register\".to_string());\n    };\n\n1659:            app.manage(paths);\n1660:            app.manage(watch);\n1661-            // Nothing is playing yet, so it starts holding nothing\n1662-            // (US-066).\n1663:            app.manage(PowerSaveBlocker::platform());\n1664-            // Nothing is playing yet, so the media keys stay with\n1665-            // whatever else on the machine wants them (US-067).\n1666:            app.manage(MediaShortcuts::global(app.handle().clone()));\n1667:            app.manage(allowlist);\n1668:            app.manage(app_menu);\n1669:            app.manage(ContextMenus::<Wry>::default());\n1670:            app.manage(FolderWatcher::default());\n1671-            // Nothing is looked for and nothing is spawned until the\n1672-            // renderer asks (US-080).\n1673:            app.manage(ExternalPlayer::system());\n1674-            // Nothing is discovered and nothing is connected until the\n1675-            // renderer asks (US-075).\n1676:            app.manage(cast::Session::default());\n1677-\n1678-            // The engine sidecar (US-070), which is\n1679-            // `engineProcess.init()` in `src/main/index.js`: it comes\n--\n1700:            app.manage(engine);\n1701-\n1702-            // `delayedInit` in `src/main/index.js`: the watcher comes\n1703-            // up with the app when the preference is already on, so\n--\n1722:        .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![\n1723-            app_paths,\n1724-            window_is_visible,\n1725-            window_is_maximized,","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PvbpbxNGdgAt7QQLi8sbBi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfyg8JVJ6Ba2WFj17cBsr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V6MxTxB8sHsxbCSHiT8H5E","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 1722,1790p src-tauri/src/main.rs; cat src-tauri/src/dock.rs | sed -n 1,60p; grep -n \"cfg(test)\" -A30 src-tauri/src/dock.rs | head -50; cat src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md | head -80"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7116,"cache_read_input_tokens":53853,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7116},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67494884-68da-4440-a200-b8d24d5f1da3","uuid":"3567211d-14f6-48cf-adb8-af9d9e15ad7b","timestamp":"2026-10-13T05:21:48.021Z","request_id":"req_011Cfyg8JEQg1RHE24UCxfPs","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01V6MxTxB8sHsxbCSHiT8H5E":{"command":"sed -n 1722,1790p src-tauri/src/main.rs; cat src-tauri/src/dock.rs | sed -n 1,60p; grep -n \"cfg(test)\" -A30 src-tauri/src/dock.rs | head -50; cat src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md | head -80"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V6MxTxB8sHsxbCSHiT8H5E","type":"tool_result","content":"        .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![\n            app_paths,\n            window_is_visible,\n            window_is_maximized,\n            window_bounds,\n            set_title,\n            set_progress,\n            set_bounds,\n            set_aspect_ratio,\n            toggle_full_screen,\n            show,\n            show_open_dialog,\n            show_save_dialog,\n            show_context_menu,\n            set_allow_nav,\n            notify_player_open,\n            notify_player_close,\n            notify_player_play,\n            notify_player_pause,\n            notify_player_update,\n            set_badge,\n            download_finished,\n            start_folder_watcher,\n            stop_folder_watcher,\n            check_for_external_player,\n            open_external_player,\n            quit_external_player,\n            get_engine_endpoint,\n            set_startup,\n            open_external,\n            open_path,\n            show_item_in_folder,\n            move_item_to_trash,\n            clipboard_read_text,\n            clipboard_write_text,\n            fs_read_file,\n            fs_write_file,\n            fs_copy_file,\n            fs_mkdir,\n            fs_rename,\n            fs_unlink,\n            fs_readdir,\n            fs_stat,\n            fs_exists,\n            cast_start_discovery,\n            cast_stop_discovery,\n            cast_get_devices,\n            cast_connect,\n            cast_play,\n            cast_pause,\n            cast_stop,\n            cast_seek,\n            cast_set_volume,\n            cast_set_rate,\n            cast_get_status\n        ])\n        .build(tauri::generate_context!())\n        .expect(\"failed to start the WebTorrent Desktop window\")\n        .run(|app, event| match event {\n            // Whatever asked for the exit -- the menu, the tray, the\n            // OS -- the app is quitting from here on, which is what\n            // the window's close handler needs to know.\n            tauri::RunEvent::ExitRequested { .. } => {\n                QUITTING.store(true, Ordering::SeqCst);\n            }\n            // Take the tray icon down with the app rather than leaving\n            // it for the notification area to notice, and give back the\n            // display-sleep block if one is still held (US-066) and the\n            // media keys if the player was still open (US-067).\n//! The dock badge and the Downloads stack.\n//!\n//! This is the Rust half of `src/main/dock.js`. It follows\n//! [`crate::menu`] and [`crate::tray`]: what a badge count *means* on\n//! a given platform is a pure function of plain values, asserted for\n//! all three platforms from one `cargo test` run, and the part that\n//! touches the world is the bottom of the file plus the commands in\n//! `main.rs`.\n//!\n//! The third thing `src/main/dock.js` does -- the dock's right-click\n//! menu, its `init` -- is not here. Tauri 2 exposes no equivalent of\n//! `app.dock.setMenu`; reaching it would mean implementing\n//! `applicationDockMenu:` on the AppKit delegate, which is a larger\n//! piece of work than this story's acceptance criteria describe (they\n//! name `setBadge`, `setProgress` and `downloadFinished`). The\n//! consequence is visible only on macOS, and only to a user who\n//! right-clicks the dock icon: the three \"open a torrent\" shortcuts\n//! are missing there. Every one of them is also in the application\n//! menu, which US-061 ported.\n//!\n//! `setProgress` is the window's own -- `set_progress` in `main.rs`\n//! over [`crate::window::progress_bar`] -- because Tauri hangs the\n//! progress bar off the window rather than off the app. It landed with\n//! the rest of the window controls in US-057 and is unchanged here.\n\nuse crate::menu::Platform;\n\n/// The distributed notification macOS bounces the Downloads stack for.\n///\n/// This is what `app.dock.downloadFinished(path)` does inside\n/// Electron: it posts `com.apple.DownloadFileFinished` with the file's\n/// path as the notification object, and the Dock -- which is the\n/// observer -- animates the stack the path is in. There is no Tauri\n/// API for it, so the notification is posted directly.\n#[cfg(target_os = \"macos\")]\nconst DOWNLOAD_FINISHED_NOTIFICATION: &str = \"com.apple.DownloadFileFinished\";\n\n/// What `app.badgeCount = Number(count)` amounts to on this platform.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Badge {\n    /// Show this many, which is Tauri's `set_badge_count(Some(n))`.\n    Show(i64),\n    /// Take the badge down. Zero is how the renderer says \"nothing is\n    /// downloading\" -- `state.dock.badge || 0` in\n    /// `src/renderer/main.js` -- and Electron clears the badge for it\n    /// rather than drawing a `0`.\n    Clear,\n    /// This platform has no badge. Windows puts a small image over the\n    /// taskbar button instead (`set_overlay_icon`), which is a\n    /// different feature with a different input: Electron's\n    /// `app.badgeCount` is documented as macOS and Linux only, and\n    /// `src/main/dock.js` guards on exactly that.\n    Unsupported,\n}\n\n/// `dock.setBadge(count)` as a decision over a plain number.\n///\n/// Two departures from the Electron original, both deliberate.\n///\n/// Electron guards Linux with `app.isUnityRunning()`; there is no such\n133:#[cfg(test)]\n134-mod tests {\n135-    use super::*;\n136-\n137-    /// The renderer sends `state.dock.badge || 0`: a count while\n138-    /// torrents are downloading, and `0` the moment the last one\n139-    /// stops.\n140-    #[test]\n141-    fn a_count_shows_and_zero_clears() {\n142-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Mac, 3.0), Badge::Show(3));\n143-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Mac, 1.0), Badge::Show(1));\n144-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Mac, 0.0), Badge::Clear);\n145-    }\n146-\n147-    /// Linux is not guarded on Unity the way `src/main/dock.js` guards\n148-    /// it -- see [`badge`]. It therefore takes the same path as macOS.\n149-    #[test]\n150-    fn linux_badges_like_mac() {\n151-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Linux, 3.0), Badge::Show(3));\n152-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Linux, 0.0), Badge::Clear);\n153-    }\n154-\n155-    /// Windows has no badge. `src/main/dock.js` does nothing there and\n156-    /// neither does this -- in particular it does not fall through to\n157-    /// the taskbar overlay icon, which is a different feature.\n158-    #[test]\n159-    fn windows_has_no_badge() {\n160-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Windows, 3.0), Badge::Unsupported);\n161-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Windows, 0.0), Badge::Unsupported);\n162-    }\n163-\n# `src/renderer/lib/platform/`\n\nThe platform shim: the sole source of Electron and Node access for\n`src/renderer/` (FR-8). `docs/platform-shim.md` is the contract --\nread it before changing anything here, and update it in the same\ncommit when the surface changes.\n\n## There was a second tree here until US-072\n\n**Read this section as history.** US-072 promoted `tauri/` to this\ndirectory and deleted the Electron tree, the `WT_HOST` variable and\nthe resolver that chose between them, so there is one implementation\nand everything below about *parity between two trees* no longer\napplies. What survives is the register of unported exports, now in\n`test/unit/platform-stubs.js`. The original text follows because the\nreasoning about stubs and capabilities is still current.\n\n## There were two trees here (US-052)\n\n`*.js` is the Electron implementation and `tauri/*.js` is the Tauri\none (ADR-014). Exactly one is in a given bundle: `WT_HOST=tauri`\nselects the second, and anything else -- including nothing, which is\nthe default -- selects the first. The rewrite is a `resolveId` plugin\nin `vite.config.js` over `bin/shim-resolver.js`; it matches the\nresolved *file*, so relative specifiers like the barrel's\n`./clipboard` are caught, and it skips imports made from inside\n`tauri/` so it cannot recurse.\n\nConsequences for anything done in this directory:\n\n- **Adding or renaming an export means doing it in both trees.**\n  `test/unit/platform-tauri.js` fails otherwise -- it compares export\n  names and arities across the two, reading the ESM sources rather\n  than requiring them (half of this directory only loads inside a real\n  Electron process, and the sibling test files stub `electron` in a\n  `require.cache` this one would trample).\n- **A new file here that is not a shim module** has to be added to\n  `HELPERS` in that test, or the tree-parity check reads it as drift.\n  `tauri/not-implemented.js` is the only one today.\n- **Tauri stubs throw, they do not return `undefined`.** Every one\n  calls `notImplemented(module, name)`, which throws a\n  `NotImplementedError` with `code: 'WT_TAURI_NOT_IMPLEMENTED'`. A\n  Phase 4 story replaces one module's throws with real calls and\n  leaves the rest alone.\n- **Porting an export means registering it.** `test/unit/platform-tauri.js`\n  sweeps every Tauri export expecting a not-implemented throw, so a\n  newly ported one has to go in that file's `IMPLEMENTED` map or the\n  sweep fails. The map is also what keeps a ported export from\n  silently regressing to a stub, and each entry is meant to have a\n  test file of its own (`platform-tauri-ipc-os.js` is the first).\n- **The Tauri host arrives through `@tauri-apps/api`**, and it is\n  banned outside this directory exactly as `electron` is -- by\n  `eslint-boundary.config.js` (as a `patterns` entry, because it is\n  reached by subpath) and by the source scan in\n  `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`. In a unit test it is stubbed\n  through `require.cache` at `require.resolve('@tauri-apps/api/event')`,\n  the same route `electron` uses.\n- **A host *event* needs a Tauri capability.** Listener exports are\n  `listen()`/`once()` from `@tauri-apps/api/event`, which the core\n  permission set covers -- `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json`. A\n  missing grant is a runtime permission error, not a build failure.\n- **`tauri/ipc-engine.js` is a re-export, not a stub**, because the\n  module has been host-free since US-044. Do not \"finish\" it.\n- **Nothing outside this directory may name `platform/tauri`.** Both\n  the lint config and `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` ban it.\n\n## Rules every module follows\n\n- **Every export is `async`** (FR-10), including wrappers around\n  Electron APIs that are synchronous today. A future host has to cross\n  a process boundary; callers must not need a second refactor.\n- **No channel string reaches a caller.** One named function per\n  channel. The dynamic engine channels take the info hash as an\n  argument.\n- **Listener registration resolves to an unsubscribe function** and\n  takes an optional `{ once: true }`. Nothing outside this directory\n  calls `removeListener`.\n- **`ipc-os.js` and `ipc-engine.js` share no exports** (FR-9).\n  `ipc-engine.js` is `wt-*` and only `wt-*`; `ipc-os.js` is everything\n  else. Adding a `wt-` channel to `ipc-os.js` breaks the seam the","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67494884-68da-4440-a200-b8d24d5f1da3","uuid":"5cb0bb72-ec32-49e3-855f-03ac0b8f95f6","timestamp":"2026-10-13T05:21:48.128Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"        .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![\n            app_paths,\n            window_is_visible,\n            window_is_maximized,\n            window_bounds,\n            set_title,\n            set_progress,\n            set_bounds,\n            set_aspect_ratio,\n            toggle_full_screen,\n            show,\n            show_open_dialog,\n            show_save_dialog,\n            show_context_menu,\n            set_allow_nav,\n            notify_player_open,\n            notify_player_close,\n            notify_player_play,\n            notify_player_pause,\n            notify_player_update,\n            set_badge,\n            download_finished,\n            start_folder_watcher,\n            stop_folder_watcher,\n            check_for_external_player,\n            open_external_player,\n            quit_external_player,\n            get_engine_endpoint,\n            set_startup,\n            open_external,\n            open_path,\n            show_item_in_folder,\n            move_item_to_trash,\n            clipboard_read_text,\n            clipboard_write_text,\n            fs_read_file,\n            fs_write_file,\n            fs_copy_file,\n            fs_mkdir,\n            fs_rename,\n            fs_unlink,\n            fs_readdir,\n            fs_stat,\n            fs_exists,\n            cast_start_discovery,\n            cast_stop_discovery,\n            cast_get_devices,\n            cast_connect,\n            cast_play,\n            cast_pause,\n            cast_stop,\n            cast_seek,\n            cast_set_volume,\n            cast_set_rate,\n            cast_get_status\n        ])\n        .build(tauri::generate_context!())\n        .expect(\"failed to start the WebTorrent Desktop window\")\n        .run(|app, event| match event {\n            // Whatever asked for the exit -- the menu, the tray, the\n            // OS -- the app is quitting from here on, which is what\n            // the window's close handler needs to know.\n            tauri::RunEvent::ExitRequested { .. } => {\n                QUITTING.store(true, Ordering::SeqCst);\n            }\n            // Take the tray icon down with the app rather than leaving\n            // it for the notification area to notice, and give back the\n            // display-sleep block if one is still held (US-066) and the\n            // media keys if the player was still open (US-067).\n//! The dock badge and the Downloads stack.\n//!\n//! This is the Rust half of `src/main/dock.js`. It follows\n//! [`crate::menu`] and [`crate::tray`]: what a badge count *means* on\n//! a given platform is a pure function of plain values, asserted for\n//! all three platforms from one `cargo test` run, and the part that\n//! touches the world is the bottom of the file plus the commands in\n//! `main.rs`.\n//!\n//! The third thing `src/main/dock.js` does -- the dock's right-click\n//! menu, its `init` -- is not here. Tauri 2 exposes no equivalent of\n//! `app.dock.setMenu`; reaching it would mean implementing\n//! `applicationDockMenu:` on the AppKit delegate, which is a larger\n//! piece of work than this story's acceptance criteria describe (they\n//! name `setBadge`, `setProgress` and `downloadFinished`). The\n//! consequence is visible only on macOS, and only to a user who\n//! right-clicks the dock icon: the three \"open a torrent\" shortcuts\n//! are missing there. Every one of them is also in the application\n//! menu, which US-061 ported.\n//!\n//! `setProgress` is the window's own -- `set_progress` in `main.rs`\n//! over [`crate::window::progress_bar`] -- because Tauri hangs the\n//! progress bar off the window rather than off the app. It landed with\n//! the rest of the window controls in US-057 and is unchanged here.\n\nuse crate::menu::Platform;\n\n/// The distributed notification macOS bounces the Downloads stack for.\n///\n/// This is what `app.dock.downloadFinished(path)` does inside\n/// Electron: it posts `com.apple.DownloadFileFinished` with the file's\n/// path as the notification object, and the Dock -- which is the\n/// observer -- animates the stack the path is in. There is no Tauri\n/// API for it, so the notification is posted directly.\n#[cfg(target_os = \"macos\")]\nconst DOWNLOAD_FINISHED_NOTIFICATION: &str = \"com.apple.DownloadFileFinished\";\n\n/// What `app.badgeCount = Number(count)` amounts to on this platform.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum Badge {\n    /// Show this many, which is Tauri's `set_badge_count(Some(n))`.\n    Show(i64),\n    /// Take the badge down. Zero is how the renderer says \"nothing is\n    /// downloading\" -- `state.dock.badge || 0` in\n    /// `src/renderer/main.js` -- and Electron clears the badge for it\n    /// rather than drawing a `0`.\n    Clear,\n    /// This platform has no badge. Windows puts a small image over the\n    /// taskbar button instead (`set_overlay_icon`), which is a\n    /// different feature with a different input: Electron's\n    /// `app.badgeCount` is documented as macOS and Linux only, and\n    /// `src/main/dock.js` guards on exactly that.\n    Unsupported,\n}\n\n/// `dock.setBadge(count)` as a decision over a plain number.\n///\n/// Two departures from the Electron original, both deliberate.\n///\n/// Electron guards Linux with `app.isUnityRunning()`; there is no such\n133:#[cfg(test)]\n134-mod tests {\n135-    use super::*;\n136-\n137-    /// The renderer sends `state.dock.badge || 0`: a count while\n138-    /// torrents are downloading, and `0` the moment the last one\n139-    /// stops.\n140-    #[test]\n141-    fn a_count_shows_and_zero_clears() {\n142-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Mac, 3.0), Badge::Show(3));\n143-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Mac, 1.0), Badge::Show(1));\n144-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Mac, 0.0), Badge::Clear);\n145-    }\n146-\n147-    /// Linux is not guarded on Unity the way `src/main/dock.js` guards\n148-    /// it -- see [`badge`]. It therefore takes the same path as macOS.\n149-    #[test]\n150-    fn linux_badges_like_mac() {\n151-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Linux, 3.0), Badge::Show(3));\n152-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Linux, 0.0), Badge::Clear);\n153-    }\n154-\n155-    /// Windows has no badge. `src/main/dock.js` does nothing there and\n156-    /// neither does this -- in particular it does not fall through to\n157-    /// the taskbar overlay icon, which is a different feature.\n158-    #[test]\n159-    fn windows_has_no_badge() {\n160-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Windows, 3.0), Badge::Unsupported);\n161-        assert_eq!(badge(Platform::Windows, 0.0), Badge::Unsupported);\n162-    }\n163-\n# `src/renderer/lib/platform/`\n\nThe platform shim: the sole source of Electron and Node access for\n`src/renderer/` (FR-8). `docs/platform-shim.md` is the contract --\nread it before changing anything here, and update it in the same\ncommit when the surface changes.\n\n## There was a second tree here until US-072\n\n**Read this section as history.** US-072 promoted `tauri/` to this\ndirectory and deleted the Electron tree, the `WT_HOST` variable and\nthe resolver that chose between them, so there is one implementation\nand everything below about *parity between two trees* no longer\napplies. What survives is the register of unported exports, now in\n`test/unit/platform-stubs.js`. The original text follows because the\nreasoning about stubs and capabilities is still current.\n\n## There were two trees here (US-052)\n\n`*.js` is the Electron implementation and `tauri/*.js` is the Tauri\none (ADR-014). Exactly one is in a given bundle: `WT_HOST=tauri`\nselects the second, and anything else -- including nothing, which is\nthe default -- selects the first. The rewrite is a `resolveId` plugin\nin `vite.config.js` over `bin/shim-resolver.js`; it matches the\nresolved *file*, so relative specifiers like the barrel's\n`./clipboard` are caught, and it skips imports made from inside\n`tauri/` so it cannot recurse.\n\nConsequences for anything done in this directory:\n\n- **Adding or renaming an export means doing it in both trees.**\n  `test/unit/platform-tauri.js` fails otherwise -- it compares export\n  names and arities across the two, reading the ESM sources rather\n  than requiring them (half of this directory only loads inside a real\n  Electron process, and the sibling test files stub `electron` in a\n  `require.cache` this one would trample).\n- **A new file here that is not a shim module** has to be added to\n  `HELPERS` in that test, or the tree-parity check reads it as drift.\n  `tauri/not-implemented.js` is the only one today.\n- **Tauri stubs throw, they do not return `undefined`.** Every one\n  calls `notImplemented(module, name)`, which throws a\n  `NotImplementedError` with `code: 'WT_TAURI_NOT_IMPLEMENTED'`. A\n  Phase 4 story replaces one module's throws with real calls and\n  leaves the rest alone.\n- **Porting an export means registering it.** `test/unit/platform-tauri.js`\n  sweeps every Tauri export expecting a not-implemented throw, so a\n  newly ported one has to go in that file's `IMPLEMENTED` map or the\n  sweep fails. The map is also what keeps a ported export from\n  silently regressing to a stub, and each entry is meant to have a\n  test file of its own (`platform-tauri-ipc-os.js` is the first).\n- **The Tauri host arrives through `@tauri-apps/api`**, and it is\n  banned outside this directory exactly as `electron` is -- by\n  `eslint-boundary.config.js` (as a `patterns` entry, because it is\n  reached by subpath) and by the source scan in\n  `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`. In a unit test it is stubbed\n  through `require.cache` at `require.resolve('@tauri-apps/api/event')`,\n  the same route `electron` uses.\n- **A host *event* needs a Tauri capability.** Listener exports are\n  `listen()`/`once()` from `@tauri-apps/api/event`, which the core\n  permission set covers -- `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json`. A\n  missing grant is a runtime permission error, not a build failure.\n- **`tauri/ipc-engine.js` is a re-export, not a stub**, because the\n  module has been host-free since US-044. Do not \"finish\" it.\n- **Nothing outside this directory may name `platform/tauri`.** Both\n  the lint config and `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` ban it.\n\n## Rules every module follows\n\n- **Every export is `async`** (FR-10), including wrappers around\n  Electron APIs that are synchronous today. A future host has to cross\n  a process boundary; callers must not need a second refactor.\n- **No channel string reaches a caller.** One named function per\n  channel. The dynamic engine channels take the info hash as an\n  argument.\n- **Listener registration resolves to an unsubscribe function** and\n  takes an optional `{ once: true }`. Nothing outside this directory\n  calls `removeListener`.\n- **`ipc-os.js` and `ipc-engine.js` share no exports** (FR-9).\n  `ipc-engine.js` is `wt-*` and only `wt-*`; `ipc-os.js` is everything\n  else. Adding a `wt-` channel to `ipc-os.js` breaks the seam the","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01V6MxTxB8sHsxbCSHiT8H5E","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
